<stack style="position: relative; width: {{ dsx.attribute.width }}; flex: 0 0 auto;
              margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto;
              padding: {{ dsx.attribute.mockup ? '0px' : dsx.attribute.bezel }};
              border-radius: {{ dsx.attribute.mockup ? '0px' : dsx.attribute.radius }};
              background: {{ dsx.attribute.mockup ? 'none' : 'linear-gradient(148deg, #55555f 0%, #2a2a31 6%, #121216 16%, #0c0c10 50%, #101015 84%, #2b2b33 94%, #52525c 100%)' }};
              box-shadow: {{ dsx.attribute.mockup ? 'none' : '0 0 0 0.5px rgba(255,255,255,0.16), 0 1px 2px rgba(0,0,0,0.5), 0 18px 34px -10px rgba(0,0,0,0.55), 0 46px 88px -26px rgba(0,0,0,0.55)' }};
              transform: {{ frameTransform }}; transform-origin: center center">
  <head>
    <!-- THE DEVICE FRAME, ONCE. Every template composes this rather than redrawing a bezel:
         five templates with five hand-drawn bezels drift, and the drift ships as five slightly
         different products.

         GENERIC BY LAW (09 §6): no Apple industrial-design cues, no logo, no Dynamic Island
         geometry. Shipping a photoreal iPhone bezel to thousands of customers is Despia's
         legal exposure, not the customer's. `mockup=` is the escape hatch for a studio that
         has licensed its own frame art, and when it is supplied the DRAWN CHROME TURNS OFF
         COMPLETELY - background, shadow, padding and radius all go, or the drawn bezel paints
         a dark rectangle behind someone else's artwork.

         THE ASPECT LIVES ON THE SCREEN, NOT ON THE FRAME. This is why the first version had a
         visible gap above and below the screenshot: aspect-ratio was on the OUTER box, the
         bezel padding was then subtracted from BOTH axes, so the inner box was no longer
         440:956 and `contain` letterboxed it - measured at 5.3px top and bottom. Sizing the
         screen and letting the frame be screen-plus-bezel makes the gap arithmetically
         impossible rather than tuned away.

         THE EDGE IS A GRADIENT, NOT A HIGHLIGHT BAR. The first version drew
         `inset 0 2px 0 rgba(255,255,255,.10)`, which on a near-black bezel reads as a pale bar
         across the top - a defect, not a material. A real anodised edge catches light at the
         corners and goes dark along the flats, which is what the 148deg ramp does; the 0.5px
         outer ring is the specular line where the edge meets the air. -->
    <!-- THE FRAME CENTRES ITSELF. `justify-content`/`align-items` written into a caller's
         style attribute are folded into a generated class that loses to the element layer's
         own rule, so every caller that "centred" the frame was silently laying it flush left -
         measured at 21.5px off in a 360pt column. Auto margins are the one form that survives
         that fold, and putting them HERE means a caller cannot get it wrong. -->
    <attribute as="screen"/>
    <attribute as="width" default="'66%'"/>
    <attribute as="radius" default="'46px'"/>
    <attribute as="bezel" default="'4px'"/>
    <attribute as="tilt" default="'0deg'"/>
    <attribute as="lift" default="'0.55'"/>
    <attribute as="label" default="''"/>
    <!-- A STUDIO'S OWN FRAME ART: a transparent-screen PNG the project supplies and has the
         right to use, plus where its screen window sits. `screenRadius` matters more than it
         looks - a screenshot has square corners, so without it the corners poke out past a
         rounded window and the frame reads as broken. -->
    <!-- THE DEFAULT IS THE RENDERED DEVICE ART, not the drawn bezel. A CSS rounded rectangle
         cannot be a device: border-radius draws a CIRCULAR corner where every phone shipped
         this decade uses a squircle, a gradient border has no thickness so the chassis has no
         edge, and one box-shadow is a blur rather than a shadow. `make-device-frame.mjs`
         renders the real geometry - superellipse corners, a chassis with a specular rail and
         an occluded underside, a bezel stepping down to the glass, buttons on the silhouette,
         3x supersampled - and the shot composites the screenshot through the transparent
         window. Pass mockup="" to fall back to the drawn bezel; pass your own licensed art
         with its own measurements to use that instead.

         screenRadius is 0 ON PURPOSE here: the art's bezel is opaque in the corner region
         between the squircle window and its bounding rectangle, so it masks the screenshot's
         square corners itself. A radius on the screen would fight the art. -->
    <attribute as="mockup" default="'device-ios.png'"/>
    <attribute as="screenTop" default="'2.860%'"/>
    <attribute as="screenLeft" default="'5.823%'"/>
    <attribute as="screenWidth" default="'88.353%'"/>
    <attribute as="screenHeight" default="'94.280%'"/>
    <attribute as="screenRadius" default="'0px'"/>
    <!-- THE ART'S OWN ASPECT, which is NOT the screen's: the art carries bezel, rail and a
         margin for the buttons to protrude into. The screen* percentages are measured against
         the ART, so the box they are positioned in must be the art's shape or every inset is
         read against the wrong denominator and the screen sits off-centre inside the body. -->
    <attribute as="artAspect" default="'498 / 1014'"/>
    <!-- THE LEAN: a REAL 3D rotation, not a flat skew. `transform` takes the CSS
         perspective() function, so the device rotates about its own Y axis with genuine
         foreshortening - the receding edge narrows, the near edge grows, and the parallel
         rails stop being parallel. That is the difference between "turned" and "tilted",
         and it is what the professional sets do when a phone enters from the side. There
         is no 3D engine here and none is needed: perspective is a projection, and CSS
         does the projection. What CSS canNOT do is bend the glass - a cylindrical curve
         needs a mesh - so we do not fake one; the lean plus the raking specular reads as
         a real object without claiming geometry we cannot render. -->
    <attribute as="lean" default="'0deg'"/>
    <attribute as="leanDepth" default="'1500px'"/>
    <!-- THE SPECULAR SWEEP: one raking highlight across the cover glass plus a falloff on
         the receding side, in a single overlay clipped to the screen. Light on glass is
         what tells the eye "this is an object", and clipping it to the screen keeps it
         honest - it reflects on the display, it does not paint over the app's content
         with a haze. -->
    <attribute as="shine" default="'false'"/>

    <variable as="frameTransform" computed="true">
      const tilt = 'rotate(' + dsx.attribute.tilt + ')'
      const lean = dsx.attribute.lean
      if (!lean || lean == '0deg') { return tilt }
      return 'perspective(' + dsx.attribute.leanDepth + ') rotateY(' + lean + ') ' + tilt
    </variable>
    <!-- A leaning device catches the light on its NEAR edge: the sweep flips with the
         rotation direction so the highlight always faces the notional light, which is what
         keeps two leaning slides in one strip looking like the same scene. -->
    <variable as="shineAngle" computed="true">
      if (dsx.attribute.lean.indexOf('-') == 0) { return '104deg' }
      return '256deg'
    </variable>
  </head>

  <!-- The screen well: black under the pixels, and `overflow: hidden` so the corners are
       clipped by the well rather than by a radius on the image, which keeps the rounding
       exact at any scale. -->
  <stack visible-if="!dsx.attribute.mockup" id="shot-screen"
         style="position: relative; width: 100%; overflow: hidden; background: #000;
                border-radius: calc({{ dsx.attribute.radius }} - {{ dsx.attribute.bezel }})">
    <image src="{{ dsx.attribute.screen }}" contentFit="cover" a11yLabel="{{ dsx.attribute.label }}"
           style="width: 100%; height: auto; aspect-ratio: 440 / 956"/>
    <!-- The glass edge: a hairline darkening where the cover glass meets the bezel. Without it
         a bright app screen butts flat against the frame and the device reads as a coloured
         rectangle with a border rather than as an object with depth. One inset hairline, not a
         vignette - anything heavier tints the app content, and the app content is the thing a
         reviewer compares against the real build. -->
    <stack style="position: absolute; top: 0; left: 0; right: 0; bottom: 0; z-index: 1;
                  border-radius: inherit;
                  box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 0.5px rgba(0,0,0,0.55),
                              inset 0 0.5px 1.5px rgba(0,0,0,0.35)"/>
    <stack visible-if="dsx.attribute.shine == 'true'"
           style="position: absolute; top: 0; left: 0; right: 0; bottom: 0; z-index: 2;
                  border-radius: inherit;
                  background: linear-gradient({{ shineAngle }},
                              rgba(255,255,255,0.20) 0%, rgba(255,255,255,0.07) 16%,
                              rgba(255,255,255,0.00) 34%, rgba(8,10,18,0.03) 68%,
                              rgba(8,10,18,0.10) 100%)"/>
  </stack>

  <!-- The supplied-art path: the screenshot sits in the declared window, clipped to the
       window's own radius, and the frame art lays over it. -->
  <!-- drop-shadow, not box-shadow: it follows the ART'S ALPHA, so the shadow has the phone's
       silhouette (buttons included) instead of the shape of a rectangle. Two passes - a tight
       contact shadow and a wide ambient one - because a single blur reads as a sticker. -->
  <stack visible-if="dsx.attribute.mockup"
         style="position: relative; width: 100%; aspect-ratio: {{ dsx.attribute.artAspect }};
                filter: drop-shadow(0 2px 3px rgba(0,0,0,0.45))
                        drop-shadow(0 26px 34px rgba(0,0,0,{{ dsx.attribute.lift }}))">
    <!-- the screenshot sits in the declared window, BEHIND the art -->
    <stack id="shot-screen" style="position: absolute; top: {{ dsx.attribute.screenTop }};
                  left: {{ dsx.attribute.screenLeft }}; width: {{ dsx.attribute.screenWidth }};
                  height: {{ dsx.attribute.screenHeight }}; overflow: hidden; background: #000;
                  border-radius: {{ dsx.attribute.screenRadius }}">
      <image src="{{ dsx.attribute.screen }}" contentFit="cover" a11yLabel="{{ dsx.attribute.label }}"
             style="width: 100%; height: 100%"/>
      <stack visible-if="dsx.attribute.shine == 'true'"
             style="position: absolute; top: 0; left: 0; right: 0; bottom: 0; z-index: 1;
                    border-radius: inherit;
                    background: linear-gradient({{ shineAngle }},
                                rgba(255,255,255,0.20) 0%, rgba(255,255,255,0.07) 16%,
                                rgba(255,255,255,0.00) 34%, rgba(8,10,18,0.03) 68%,
                                rgba(8,10,18,0.10) 100%)"/>
    </stack>
    <image src="{{ dsx.attribute.mockup }}" contentFit="contain" a11yLabel=""
           style="position: relative; width: 100%; height: auto; z-index: 1"/>
  </stack>
</stack>
